# Google Go Style

Follow [Google's Go Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/go/). Run `gofmt` and `go vet` on every change. Go has no Python-style type hints or docstrings — use explicit types and GoDoc instead.

## Naming

- MixedCaps; never_underscores in identifiers.
- Initialisms stay caps: `URL`, `ID`, `HTTP` (not `Url`, `Id`).
- Package names: short, lowercase, singular, no `util`/`common` dumping grounds.
- No stutter: `exec.Runner`, not `exec.ExecRunner`.
- Receivers: 1–2 letter names (`r *Runner`), never `this`/`self`.

## Explicit types

```go
// ❌ BAD — any / unnamed structure
func Run(opts map[string]any) error

// ✅ GOOD — named types at the API boundary
type RunOptions struct {
    Dir     string
    Timeout time.Duration
}
func Run(ctx context.Context, opts RunOptions) error
```

Avoid `any` / `interface{}` unless a real untyped boundary exists. Prefer `context.Context` as the first parameter on blocking/I/O calls. Do not store `ctx` on structs.

## GoDoc (Google style)

Document every exported name. Comments are complete sentences that start with the name.

```go
// Package pipeline loads and validates pipeline definitions.
package pipeline

// Step is a single executable unit in a pipeline.
type Step struct {
    Name string
    Cmd  []string
}

// Validate reports whether s is well-formed.
func (s Step) Validate() error { /* ... */ }
```

Unexported APIs get comments only when the behavior is non-obvious. Keep comments in English, wrapped near 80 columns. Do not HTML-format comments.
